// Running one case: fork, run it in the child with its limits, and read back
// what it wrote to stderr and the two counts it measured. The child first
// enters idtc_dir[k], the root of the source tree the function under test
// belongs to (judge/dirs.id), so a case reads files relative to its own
// library wherever that library is imported from; with no entry it stays
// where bin/idc started the harness, the project root.
//
// With $IDTC_READS, a directory, the child also sets $IDTC_READLOG to
// $IDTC_READS/k, the file idstd's fs backend (host/io/fs/fs_posix.c) appends
// every path the case reads or probes to; bin/idc turns those files into the
// read sets of the cases keyed `KEY f` (compiler/parse/conf.id).
//
// A case function returns 0 when it passed, having written its counts; 3 when
// its result did not match, having printed the message; anything else, or a
// signal, is the case stopping on its own -- a runtime trap, a crash, a limit.
//
// idtc_stdin_set (unit/more/text/proc.id) runs before idtc_limit, in the
// child, for every case -- not only one with a `stdin` clause -- so a case
// never sees the harness's own stdin or the terminal: idtc_in[k] is `""` by
// default (judge/more/row.id).
//
// Teardown is the harness's, so a case writes none. Before the fork the parent
// makes an empty /tmp/idcase_XXXXXX; the child gets it as $TMPDIR (where
// fs_mktemp puts every scratch name), leads a process group of its own, and
// once the child has ended -- passed, failed, trapped, killed by its time
// limit -- the parent kills (kill(-pid)) that whole group, so nothing the case started
// survives, and removes the directory with everything in it. The child is
// waited for without being reaped until the group is dead, so its pid cannot
// be reused for the group in between. A child the case left running may still
// hold the stderr pipe open, so the parent reads it only while the case is
// alive (idtc_pull, proc.id), and drains what is left once the group is dead,
// rather than waiting for an end of file that child would never send. The
// removal and the reading live in interop/idtc_teardown.c, an object bin/idc
// links into the harness: the libc headers they need would otherwise declare
// names a program's global variables may be called.

emit_tc_support() {
  print((import c_tc_proc));
  print((import c_tc_one));
  print((import c_tc_judge));
} return void;
